-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/fb3c51a4-ea81-4f74-b850-c0b03cb2eafe
-- title:
--   The greedy sparse-simplex method (§3.2): one general step and runs of the method
-- statement:
--   The **greedy sparse-simplex method** for (P) starts at a point $x^0\in C_s$ and, at iteration $k=0,1,\dots$, does the following.
--
--   1. If $\|x^k\|_0<s$: for every $i$ compute $t_i\in\operatorname{argmin}_{t\in\mathbb R}f(x^k+te_i)$ and $f_i=\min_t f(x^k+te_i)$ (3.4). Let $i_k\in\operatorname{argmin}_i f_i$. If $f_{i_k}<f(x^k)$, set $x^{k+1}=x^k+t_{i_k}e_{i_k}$; otherwise STOP.
--   2. If $\|x^k\|_0=s$: for every $i\in I_1(x^k)$ and every $j$ compute $t_{i,j}\in\operatorname{argmin}_{t}f(x^k-x^k_ie_i+te_j)$ and $f_{i,j}=\min_t f(x^k-x^k_ie_i+te_j)$ (3.5). Let $(i_k,j_k)\in\operatorname{argmin}\{f_{i,j}: i\in I_1(x^k),\ j=1,\dots,n\}$. If $f_{i_k,j_k}<f(x^k)$, set $x^{k+1}=x^k-x^k_{i_k}e_{i_k}+t_{i_k,j_k}e_{j_k}$; otherwise STOP.
--
--   A sequence $(x^k)_{k\ge0}$ is a **run** of the method if $x^0\in C_s$ and each $x^{k+1}$ arises from $x^k$ by this rule, where a STOP leaves the sequence at the stopping point: $x^{k+1}=x^k$. Concretely, when $\|x^k\|_0<s$, either $x^{k+1}=x^k+te_i$ for some $(i,t)$ with $f(x^{k+1})<f(x^k)$ and
--   $$f(x^{k+1})\le f(x^k+t'e_j)\quad\text{for all } j \text{ and } t'\in\mathbb R,$$
--   or $f(x^k)\le f(x^k+t'e_j)$ for all $j,t'$ and $x^{k+1}=x^k$; when $\|x^k\|_0=s$, the same alternative holds with the candidate points $x^k-x^k_{i'}e_{i'}+t'e_{j'}$, $i'\in I_1(x^k)$, $j'$ arbitrary, $t'\in\mathbb R$.
--
--   The method greedily performs the best single-coordinate move (below the sparsity level) or the best swap of a support coordinate (at the sparsity level), and its accumulation points are the subject of Theorem 3.3.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The argmin selections $t_i$, $i_k$, $t_{i,j}$, $(i_k,j_k)$ are encoded by requiring that $x^{k+1}$ attains the minimum of $f$ over all candidate points; since the run is any sequence satisfying this, every theorem about runs holds for every choice of minimizers. The box presupposes that the coordinate minima are attained; the predicate requires attainment only for the move actually taken, which allows at least the paper's runs. "Otherwise, STOP" ($f_{i_k}\ge f(x^k)$) is written as $f(x^k)\le f(\text{candidate})$ for every candidate, and a STOP is modelled as $x^{k+1}=x^k$ (the convention of Lemma 3.2). Indices are $0,\dots,n-1$ and $te_i$ is `EuclideanSpace.single i t`.
-- source:
--   Beck, Eldar, Sparsity Constrained Nonlinear Optimization: Optimality Conditions and Algorithms, SIAM J. Optim. (2013), doi:10.1137/120869778 (source: arXiv:1203.4580v1), p. 19, §3.2, the greedy sparse-simplex method, (3.4), (3.5)

import Definitions.Def_SparseNLO_CWOpt_Setting

namespace SparseNLO.Greedy

/-- One general step of the greedy sparse-simplex method (box, p. 19) from `xk` to `xk1`.

* If `‖xᵏ‖₀ < s`: either (move) `xᵏ⁺¹ = xᵏ + t eᵢ` for some `i, t` with `f(xᵏ⁺¹) < f(xᵏ)` and
  `f(xᵏ⁺¹) ≤ f(xᵏ + t' eⱼ)` for every `j` and `t'` (so `(i, t)` realizes `t_{i_k}` and `i_k` of
  (3.4)); or (STOP) `f(xᵏ) ≤ f(xᵏ + t' eⱼ)` for every `j, t'` (i.e. `f_{i_k} ≥ f(xᵏ)`), and then
  `xᵏ⁺¹ = xᵏ`.
* If `‖xᵏ‖₀ = s`: either (move) `xᵏ⁺¹ = xᵏ − xᵏᵢ eᵢ + t eⱼ` for some `i ∈ I₁(xᵏ)`, `j`, `t`
  with `f(xᵏ⁺¹) < f(xᵏ)` and `f(xᵏ⁺¹) ≤ f(xᵏ − xᵏᵢ' eᵢ' + t' eⱼ')` for every `i' ∈ I₁(xᵏ)`, `j'`,
  `t'` (so `(i, j, t)` realizes `(i_k, j_k)` and `t_{i_k, j_k}` of (3.5)); or (STOP)
  `f(xᵏ) ≤ f(xᵏ − xᵏᵢ' eᵢ' + t' eⱼ')` for every such `i', j', t'`, and then `xᵏ⁺¹ = xᵏ`.

A STOP is modelled by the sequence staying at the stopping point. -/
def GreedyStep {n : ℕ} (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (s : ℕ)
    (xk xk1 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) : Prop :=
  (SparseNLO.CWOpt.l0 xk < s →
    ((∃ (i : Fin n) (t : ℝ), xk1 = xk + EuclideanSpace.single i t ∧ f xk1 < f xk ∧
        ∀ (j : Fin n) (t' : ℝ), f xk1 ≤ f (xk + EuclideanSpace.single j t')) ∨
     ((∀ (j : Fin n) (t' : ℝ), f xk ≤ f (xk + EuclideanSpace.single j t')) ∧ xk1 = xk))) ∧
  (SparseNLO.CWOpt.l0 xk = s →
    ((∃ i ∈ SparseNLO.CWOpt.I1 xk, ∃ (j : Fin n) (t : ℝ),
        xk1 = xk - EuclideanSpace.single i (xk i) + EuclideanSpace.single j t ∧ f xk1 < f xk ∧
        ∀ i' ∈ SparseNLO.CWOpt.I1 xk, ∀ (j' : Fin n) (t' : ℝ),
          f xk1 ≤ f (xk - EuclideanSpace.single i' (xk i') + EuclideanSpace.single j' t')) ∨
     ((∀ i' ∈ SparseNLO.CWOpt.I1 xk, ∀ (j' : Fin n) (t' : ℝ),
          f xk ≤ f (xk - EuclideanSpace.single i' (xk i') + EuclideanSpace.single j' t')) ∧
        xk1 = xk)))

/-- A sequence `x : ℕ → ℝⁿ` generated by the greedy sparse-simplex method (box, p. 19): the
initial point lies in `C_s` and every step is a `GreedyStep`. The selections `tᵢ`, `i_k`,
`t_{i,j}`, `(i_k, j_k)` are arbitrary minimizers, so this describes every run of the method. -/
def IsGreedyRun {n : ℕ} (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (s : ℕ)
    (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) : Prop :=
  x 0 ∈ SparseNLO.CWOpt.Cs n s ∧ ∀ k : ℕ, GreedyStep f s (x k) (x (k + 1))

end SparseNLO.Greedy


